{"name":"Flounce","hex":"#4a8791","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"flounce","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To move in a bouncy, exaggerated manner.","To depart in a dramatic, haughty way that draws attention to oneself."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A strip of decorative material, usually pleated, attached along one edge; a ruffle.","The act of flouncing; a dramatic departure."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/flounce"}],"entries":[{"title":"Ruffle (sewing)","blocks":[{"text":"In sewing and dressmaking, a ruffle, frill, or furbelow is a strip of fabric, lace or ribbon tightly gathered or pleated on one edge and applied to a garment, bedding, or other textile as a form of trimming."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/80/Gullager_Salisbury.jpg/330px-Gullager_Salisbury.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ruffle_(sewing)"},{"title":"Fourth, fifth, and sixth derivatives of position","blocks":[{"text":"In the physics field of kinematics, the fourth, fifth and sixth derivatives of position are generalizations of velocity and acceleration. They are defined as derivatives of the position vector with respect to time – with the first, second, and third derivatives being velocity, acceleration, and jerk, respectively. These higher-order derivatives are less common than the first three; thus their names are not as standardized, though the concept of a minimum snap trajectory has been used in robotics."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/Time_derivatives_of_position.svg/330px-Time_derivatives_of_position.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fourth%2C_fifth%2C_and_sixth_derivatives_of_position"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/Singer_Ruffler_Attachment.jpg/500px-Singer_Ruffler_Attachment.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/Singer_Ruffler_Attachment.jpg/1280px-Singer_Ruffler_Attachment.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/Singer_Ruffler_Attachment.jpg/500px-Singer_Ruffler_Attachment.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Mechanical ruffler by Singer, used on domestic sewing machines","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASinger_Ruffler_Attachment.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Flounce_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-Flounce_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Flounce_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-Flounce_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Sketch of a flounce","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFlounce_(cropped).jpg"}],"sounds":{"flounce":[{"type":"audio/wav","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/27/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-flounce.wav?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/2/27/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-flounce.wav/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-flounce.wav.mp3"},{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/2/27/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-flounce.wav/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-flounce.wav.ogg"},{"type":"audio/wav","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8d/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Naomi_Persephone_Amethyst_%28NaomiAmethyst%29-flounce.wav?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.o